    <h1>Paprika vs Chilli Powder: What's the Difference</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      Paprika and chilli powder are both red powders from the same plant genus, and that is where the
      similarity ends. One is bought for colour and mild flavour; the other is bought for heat. This
      guide explains the difference and how to specify each correctly.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Paprika is ground <em>sweet, non-pungent</em> pepper (near 0 SHU)
      bought for its red colour and mild flavour; chilli powder is ground <em>hot</em> chilli bought for
      heat. They are not interchangeable — paprika cannot provide chilli heat.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The core difference</h2>
    <table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;margin-top:8px;">
      <thead>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #d8c9c0;text-align:left;">
          <th style="padding:8px;">Dimension</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Paprika</th>
          <th style="padding:8px;">Chilli powder</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Source</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Sweet, non-pungent pepper (Capsicum annuum)</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Hot chilli varieties</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">~0–500 (non-pungent)</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Variable — thousands to tens of thousands</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border-bottom:1px solid #eee0d8;">
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Bought for</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Colour (ASTA) + mild flavour</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">Heat + flavour</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td style="padding:8px;"><strong>Key spec</strong></td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">ASTA colour value</td>
          <td style="padding:8px;">SHU heat window</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Why the two are confused</h2>
    <p>
      Both are dried, ground red pepper powders, and in some regions "chilli powder" is used loosely to
      describe either. The confusion costs money when a buyer orders "chilli powder" expecting heat and
      receives a colour-grade paprika — or vice versa. The fix is to specify the <em>variety and the
      SHU window</em>, which removes the ambiguity entirely.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify each correctly</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Paprika</strong> — specify the <a href="/guides/asta-colour-value/">ASTA colour
      value</a> and confirm it is sweet (non-pungent), not hot.</li>
      <li><strong>Chilli powder</strong> — specify the <a href="/guides/asta-colour-and-shu-heat-explained/">SHU
      window</a>, the variety, and the mesh.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The two properties you are separating are exactly the two independent dimensions this site covers:
      <a href="/guides/asta-colour-value/">colour (ASTA)</a> and
      <a href="/guides/asta-colour-and-shu-heat-explained/">heat (SHU)</a>. Paprika optimises colour with
      no heat; chilli powder optimises heat, usually with less colour.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">When to use each</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Paprika</strong> — colour and mild sweet flavour: goulash, paprika chicken, colour
      blending, snacks, and anywhere heat is unwanted.</li>
      <li><strong>Chilli powder</strong> — heat and chilli flavour: curries, hot sauces, chilli oil,
      seasoning blends and anything scored on spiciness.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">The Author's Take</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Position:</strong> In my view, the paprika-vs-chilli-powder mix-up is a specification
      problem, not a sourcing problem — and it is solved by writing a variety and an SHU window instead
      of a generic name.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Reasoning:</strong> The two powders look alike and the names blur across regions, so the
      only reliable separation is the number: ASTA for paprika, SHU for chilli powder. A buyer who
      writes "paprika, ASTA 120, sweet" or "Sanying chilli powder, 30,000–50,000 SHU" never receives the
      wrong product. The buyer who writes "red powder" is guessing.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Disclosure:</strong> This is my editorial view as a dried-chilli supplier's technical team,
      not a universal naming rule — regional terminology varies, so always confirm the variety and the
      heat window.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is the difference between paprika and chilli powder?</h3>
      <p>Paprika is ground sweet, non-pungent pepper (near 0 SHU) bought for colour and mild flavour; chilli powder is ground hot chilli bought for heat. They are not interchangeable.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>Can I substitute paprika for chilli powder?</h3>
      <p>Not for heat — paprika has essentially no capsaicin. Use paprika for colour without heat; get chilli heat from chilli powder.</p>
    </div>

